<article id="start-here" class="start-content" tabindex="-1">
<header class="start-masthead">
<p class="docs-breadcrumb">CyberAlchemy Lab / Getting started</p>
<h1>Start here</h1>
<p class="start-deck">A first reading of the ideas that organize knowledge and work in CyberAlchemy Lab.</p>
<p class="start-reading-time">{{READING_TIME}} minute read · No prior knowledge of the Lab required</p>
</header>
<section id="organize-knowledge" aria-labelledby="organize-knowledge-title">
<h2 id="organize-knowledge-title">Organize knowledge and work</h2>
<p><strong>CyberAlchemy is a system for organizing knowledge and work.</strong> It connects what things mean, why work matters, what was done and what can happen next. People and agents can recover those connections instead of reconstructing them from scattered conversations.</p>
<p>Consider a customer reporting an incomplete data export. Investigating it requires a meaning for “complete,” access to the relevant data, a comparison that can be examined and a decision about the response. The Lab gives each of these a place in the same body of knowledge.</p>
<p>The guide follows this through the fictional <a href="governance-lab-reference.html#opening-incident-case-title">Northstar incident</a>. This introduction gives you the concepts needed to follow it.</p>
</section>
<section id="definitions-first" aria-labelledby="definitions-first-title">
<h2 id="definitions-first-title">Begin with what the words mean</h2>
<p>Definitions make the basic meanings and boundaries explicit. An <a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-definition">accepted definition</a> is a basic unit of authority over meaning: an accountable owner has accepted an exact version for a declared purpose and scope.</p>
<p>For the export investigation, “complete” means that every required row identifier from the declared account, filters and fixed source snapshot appears in the saved output. That criterion lets the agent assess identifier coverage. Accuracy of the values and permission to access the data have their own requirements.</p>
<dl class="start-roles">
<div><dt><a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-definition">Definition</a></dt><dd>States a term’s meaning and where that meaning applies.</dd></div>
<div><dt><a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-artifact-type">Artifact Type</a></dt><dd>Sets the contract for a kind of record, including its meaning bindings, format and validation requirements.</dd></div>
<div><dt><a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-artifact">Artifact</a></dt><dd>An identifiable, typed and versioned record—for example, this particular investigation’s Work record.</dd></div>
</dl>
<p>Connections between records also follow declared rules. An <a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-ontology-view">Ontology View</a> represents selected subjects and connections as a graph, preserving their identities and the source on which the view depends.</p>
<p class="start-further"><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#records">Explore meanings, record formats and connections →</a></p>
</section>
<section id="three-questions" aria-labelledby="three-questions-title">
<h2 id="three-questions-title">Ask three governance questions</h2>
<p>Authority, provenance and lifecycle make different parts of the investigation explicit.</p>
<dl class="start-lenses">
<div><dt><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#governance">Authority</a><span>What governs meaning and action?</span></dt><dd>The accepted completeness criterion governs the assessment. A separate, scoped permission determines what the agent may inspect or change.</dd></div>
<div><dt><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#evidence">Provenance</a><span>Where did this come from?</span></dt><dd>Identify the source snapshot, account, filters, output, comparison method and recorded result that support the omission claim.</dd></div>
<div><dt><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#change">Lifecycle</a><span>What can change from here?</span></dt><dd>The definition, proposed code and incident each have their own state and requirements for change. A proposed fix proceeds through its assessment and owner decision.</dd></div>
</dl>
<p>These questions stay connected throughout the work. Evidence supports a conclusion within its scope; permission governs the requested action; a recorded transition preserves the resulting state.</p>
</section>
<section id="purpose-and-work" aria-labelledby="purpose-and-work-title">
<h2 id="purpose-and-work-title">Connect the need to the work</h2>
<p>The Lab keeps the purpose, promised outcome and particular effort identifiable. Read these roles through the same incident:</p>
<ol class="start-workflow" aria-label="Roles in the incident investigation">
<li data-model-concept="intent">{{INTENT_ICON}}<div><h3>Intent <span>The need</span></h3><p>Customers need their complete eligible data. An <a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-intent-resolution">Intent Resolution</a> selects a response and how to assess satisfaction.</p></div></li>
<li data-model-concept="product">{{PRODUCT_ICON}}<div><h3>Product <span>The promise of value</span></h3><p>The customer-data platform enables exports through a Product Capability. Specific implementations realize that outcome.</p></div></li>
<li data-model-concept="project">{{PROJECT_ICON}}<div><h3>Project and Goal <span>The coordinated effort</span></h3><p>The export-repair Project organizes the effort. Its Goal states the desired outcome and the evidence needed to establish success.</p></div></li>
<li data-model-concept="work">{{WORK_ICON}}<div><h3>Work <span>The bounded job</span></h3><p>Investigate the reported omission, using named inputs, constraints and expected results.</p></div></li>
<li data-model-concept="agent">{{AGENT_ICON}}<div><h3>Agent Assignment <span>The participant’s job</span></h3><p>Bind an agent to that investigation. Establish its permission separately through the applicable authority or delegation.</p></div></li>
</ol>
<p class="start-further"><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#purpose">Follow the need into the investigation →</a></p>
</section>
<section id="agent-continuity" aria-labelledby="agent-continuity-title">
<h2 id="agent-continuity-title">Let knowledge outlast the agent session</h2>
<p>A machine agent works in a temporary <a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-agent-session">Agent Session</a>. A Context View prepares selected task information; an Ontology View provides a purpose-specific view of the connected model. Their source versions, limits and refresh conditions remain explicit.</p>
<p>Long-term continuity comes from <a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-project-state">Project State</a>: saved records, definitions, decisions, evidence, relationships and histories. Short-term working memory belongs to the session. When a machine agent session ends, its instance ends too; a later session recovers durable knowledge through newly checked views.</p>
<p>In the incident, the identifier comparison can survive the session. A later agent can inspect its basis and continue investigating the cause. A proposed lesson can also be retained, then examined through evidence of later use and benefit.</p>
<p class="start-further"><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#agent">Follow an agent’s session and handoff →</a></p>
</section>
<section id="craft-in-the-lab" aria-labelledby="craft-in-the-lab-title">
<h2 id="craft-in-the-lab-title">Use Craft for the software design</h2>
<p><strong>Craft is the software-design Product within the broader Lab model.</strong> A project can use its tools to express software responsibilities, architectural constraints and supported model-to-code construction.</p>
<p>Its model distinguishes a <strong>Rule</strong> that evaluates a condition, a <strong>Calculation</strong> that derives a value, a <strong>Query</strong> that retrieves information and an <strong>Operation</strong> that owns a business state change. In Craft’s capacity example, a Calculation returns Accept when the request is strictly below the supplied available capacity. Reserving that capacity belongs to a separate Operation.</p>
<p>The Craft software model describes the design and its implementation. The Lab connects that work with its purpose, ownership, evidence and decisions. Generated code and checks can become outputs of the work; accepting records and authorizing deployment follow their own authority routes.</p>
<p class="start-further"><a href="../craft-documentation-proposal/craft-model.html#governance">Read how Craft fits into the Lab →</a></p>
</section>
